course: dictionary
lesson: 1403
title: Words from Earth's deep past
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a time, not a thing. When you meet a new word, read the words around it for clues. «Coal formed mostly from swamp forests of the Carboniferous period, about 360-300 million years ago.» The clues are period, years ago and swamp forests, so Carboniferous must be the name of a long time in Earth's past.
explain: Now read the second sentence: «Coal is squashed swamp forest, mostly from the Carboniferous period.» The clue is coal, which is made from the swamp forest. Try your meaning in the sentence: "Coal is squashed swamp forest, mostly from a long time in Earth's past" still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
explain: Carboniferous can also be a describing word, meaning containing or producing coal. Use the words around it to tell which meaning is meant. When the context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Carboniferous is a noun for a time in Earth's history, about 359 to 299 million years ago.
fact: Carboniferous can also be an adjective, meaning containing or producing coal.
fact: During the Carboniferous, huge swamp forests grew, and later they turned into coal.
fact: In science lessons, the Carboniferous is known for giant insects, such as dragonflies as big as seagulls, and forests of giant ferns.
fact: Coal mined in Britain and elsewhere formed from plants of the Carboniferous.
exercise: «Coal formed mostly from swamp forests of the Carboniferous period.» What is the Carboniferous here? | A kind of swamp | *A long time in Earth's history | A giant dragonfly | A type of coal mine
exercise: «Coal is squashed swamp forest, mostly from the Carboniferous period.» What is coal made from in this sentence? | Squashed sea shells | Dried-out desert sand | *Squashed swamp forest | Old ice from the mountains
exercise: Carboniferous can be a describing word. What does it mean as a describing word? | Made of ice | Very hot and dry | Full of giant insects only | *Containing or producing coal
test: Scientists say giant dragonflies, as big as seagulls, lived in the Carboniferous. What was the Carboniferous? | A kind of seagull | *A time in Earth's history | A coal mine in Britain | A type of dragonfly
test: Which clue in «Coal formed mostly from swamp forests of the Carboniferous period» shows that the Carboniferous is a time? | Forests | *Period | Swamp | Coal
exam: Which word means containing or producing coal? | *Carboniferous | Fern | Seagull | Swamp
